e(9,'inspect-pandas','Inspect a CSV with pandas','Read the CSV with pandas and print its number of rows, number of columns, and first three sample IDs in file order.','One CSV filename with columns sample,group,value. It may have only a header.','`rows: <n>`, `columns: 3`, `samples: <IDs joined by spaces>`. Use `samples: none` for no rows.','import pandas as pd\nframe = pd.read_csv(input())\nprint(f"rows: {frame.shape[0]}")\nprint(f"columns: {frame.shape[1]}")\nprint("samples: " + (" ".join(frame["sample"].head(3).tolist()) or "none"))',[tc([measure_a],'rows: 6\ncolumns: 3\nsamples: s3 s1 s2',fixtures=[measure_a]),tc([measure_b],'rows: 3\ncolumns: 3\nsamples: B A C',fixtures=[measure_b]),tc([measure_c],'rows: 0\ncolumns: 3\nsamples: none',fixtures=[measure_c])],hint='Use `.shape`, select `frame["sample"]`, and `.head(3)`. Do not print the entire DataFrame.',starter='import pandas as pd\nframe = pd.read_csv(input())\n# Print scalar counts and the first IDs\n')
e(9,'select-measurements','Select and sort measurements','Keep rows where group is treated and value is at least the threshold. Sort by sample ID and print sample and value. Missing values do not pass the filter.','CSV filename on line 1; numeric threshold on line 2. CSV columns are sample,group,value. Sample IDs are unique.','`<sample>,<value to two decimals>` per selected row, no header; `none` when empty.','import pandas as pd\nframe = pd.read_csv(input())\nthreshold = float(input())\nselected = frame.loc[(frame["group"] == "treated") & (frame["value"] >= threshold)].sort_values("sample")\nfor row in selected.itertuples(index=False):\n    print(f"{row.sample},{row.value:.2f}")\nif selected.empty:\n    print("none")',[tc([measure_a,3],'s2,3.00\ns3,5.00',fixtures=[measure_a]),tc([measure_b,1.5],'A,1.50\nB,1.50',fixtures=[measure_b]),tc([measure_a,6],'none',fixtures=[measure_a])],hint='Use parentheses around each condition and `&` between them. `.loc` selects by labels/conditions; `.iloc` selects by integer position.',difficulty='Medium')
e(9,'group-missing-data','Summarise groups with missing data','Drop rows missing value. For each remaining group, print its number of rows and mean value. Sort by group. Groups with no remaining values are omitted.','One CSV filename with sample,group,value columns. Group names are non-empty.','`<group>: count=<n>, mean=<two decimals>` per remaining group; `none` when no rows remain.','import pandas as pd\nframe = pd.read_csv(input()).dropna(subset=["value"])\nsummary = frame.groupby("group", sort=True)["value"].agg(["count", "mean"])\nfor group, row in summary.iterrows():\n    print(f"{group}: count={int(row[\'count\'])}, mean={row[\'mean\']:.2f}")\nif summary.empty:\n    print("none")',[tc([measure_a],'control: count=1, mean=2.00\ntreated: count=2, mean=4.00',fixtures=[measure_a]),tc([measure_b],'control: count=1, mean=4.00\ntreated: count=2, mean=1.50',fixtures=[measure_b]),tc([measure_c],'none',fixtures=[measure_c])],hint='`.dropna(subset=["value"])` removes only missing measurements. `.groupby()` collects rows with the same group.',difficulty='Harder')
