e(10,'synthetic-edges','Detect edges in a synthetic image','Load the grayscale PNG as a floating-point array with values 0–1 using skimage.io.imread(..., as_gray=True) and img_as_float. Run feature.canny with sigma=1.0, low_threshold=0.1 and high_threshold=0.2. Print the number of true pixels, then mask rows 4 and 8 as contiguous 0/1 strings.','One small grayscale PNG filename. Rows 4 and 8 always exist.','`edges: <count>`, `row4: <bits>`, `row8: <bits>`. Each bit string has one character per image column.','from skimage import io, feature, img_as_float\nimage = img_as_float(io.imread(input(), as_gray=True))\nmask = feature.canny(image, sigma=1.0, low_threshold=0.1, high_threshold=0.2)\nprint(f"edges: {int(mask.sum())}")\nfor row in [4, 8]:\n    print(f"row{row}: " + "".join("1" if value else "0" for value in mask[row]))',[tc(['python-course/'+f],{'square-a.png':'edges: 28\nrow4: 0000111111110000\nrow8: 0000100000010000','square-b.png':'edges: 36\nrow4: 00000000000000000000\nrow8: 00000100000000100000','square-blank.png':'edges: 0\nrow4: 0000000000000000\nrow8: 0000000000000000'}[f],fixtures=['python-course/'+f]) for f in ['square-a.png','square-b.png','square-blank.png']],hint='A true mask pixel marks an edge. The synthetic examples isolate this operation; no circle detector or photograph analysis is required.',starter='from skimage import io, feature, img_as_float\nfilename = input()\n# Load, convert to floating point, compute the mask, then print\n',difficulty='Harder')

